{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788179385,"nonce":"VHEw4GH/9FaqDFAsV+xRCRu0g8Q=","message":"WORKER REPORT — 3.4 shipped on `spec/3.4-pawn-orders`, 9 commits, 35 verbs.\nOpen-question resolutions with rationale, plus five findings. Opus worker,\nsession 6, BORGES.\n\n== THE SPEC'S THREE OPEN QUESTIONS ==\n\n1. PRIORITIZE SURFACE BREADTH — resolved: DO NOT ENUMERATE IT. The spec asked\nfor \"haul/tend/repair/construct at minimum\". A hand-written list would have been\na guess: `WorkGiverDef.directOrderable` defaults TRUE, so on a 38-mod bench every\n`WorkGiver_Scanner` is a right-click order, and the set is not knowable at\ncompile time. So `prioritize {pawn, work, thing|cell}` takes ANY WorkGiverDef,\nand a new read-only verb `orders {pawn, thing?|cell?}` enumerates what the game\nwould actually offer for that pawn on that target, with the game's own labels and\nits own disabled-option strings. The agent discovers the surface instead of being\nhanded my list of four. Both forms are there — the CELL branch is a different\nprovider path (`GetOptions` with `ClickedCell` vs `GetOptionsFor(Thing)`) and is\nhow \"sow this area\" and \"mine this area\" are ordered.\n\n2. SCHEDULE BLOCK FORMAT — resolved: A SPAN, three notations, and it may WRAP.\n`hours` accepts `\"0-5\"` (inclusive), `[22,23]`, a bare hour, or `\"all\"`. A\nwrapping span is legal and is the point: `\"22-3\"` is the night watch, and\nrefusing it would make the caller issue two calls for one block. There is also\n`schedule {pawns, copy_from}` for the whole 24-hour row, mirroring\n`PawnColumnWorker_CopyPasteTimetable`. The echoed `row` is a 24-char string in\n`PawnSerializer.Schedule`'s own A/W/J/S/M legend, so a written row and a read row\nare one vocabulary rather than two.\n\n3. POLICY SPEC FORMAT — resolved: mirror 3.2's filter approach as asked, with one\ncorrection the spec could not have known. `allow`/`disallow` take defNames and\nresolve ThingDef -\u003e ThingCategoryDef -\u003e SpecialThingFilterDef (the three\n`ThingFilter.SetAllow` overloads, and the three row kinds `ThingFilterUI` draws),\nplus `allow_all`/`disallow_all` sweeps applied first so\n`{disallow_all:true, allow:[\"Apparel_Parka\"]}` reads as \"only parkas\".\n\nTHE CORRECTION, and it is the gate a plausible implementation drops: the manage\ndialog bounds its tree with a GLOBAL PARENT FILTER, and two of the three are NOT\ncategory-based.\n  apparel  Dialog_ManageApparelPolicies.ApparelGlobalFilter = the Apparel category\n  food     Dialog_ManageFoodPolicies.FoodGlobalFilter = every ThingDef with\n           Nutrition \u003e 0 -- NOT the Foods category (kibble, meals, raw meat,\n           chocolate and nutrient paste do not share one)\n  reading  Dialog_ManageReadingPolicies.PolicyGlobalFilter = every ThingDef with\n           a CompBook\n  drug     no filter at all: a drug policy is DrugPolicyEntry rows, edited with\n           `drugs:[{drug, for_joy, scheduled, days_frequency, ...}]` in 2.4's\n           field names\nAn `allow` outside the parent is refused with that reason, because the game's own\ndialog cannot express it.\n\n== RESOLUTIONS I MADE THAT THE SPEC DID NOT ASK FOR ==\n\n4. OBSERVER BANS ARE NOT PLAYER-VERB BANS, and the line matters. PawnSafe and\nWorldSafe ban accessors whose READ mutates. Those are OBSERVER bans. A player\nverb is a reproduction of a click, so where the WIDGET ITSELF calls a lazy-init\ngetter, the verb calls it too -- the player's click does\n(FloatMenuOptionProvider_RescuePawn reads\n`clickedPawn.ageTracker.CurLifeStage.alwaysDowned`; reproducing rescue's gate\nmeans reading it). What stays banned is anything ADDITIONAL to the click: the\npolicy getters that assign-and-scribe (reads go through PawnSafe.Policies, writes\nthrough the public setter), GetPriority/SetPriority ungated by EverWork,\nIsFinished/CanStartNow/AvailableNow, and\nEffectiveAreaRestrictionInPawnCurrentMap. Stated in PawnActs.cs's header. If the\norchestrator disagrees, the whole file's discipline turns on this one paragraph.\n\n5. ONE WIDGET CLAUSE DELIBERATELY NOT REPRODUCED. MainTabWindow_Work and\nMainTabWindow_Assign filter their roster with `!pawn.DevelopmentalStage.Baby()`,\nand `Pawn.DevelopmentalStage` routes through `Pawn_AgeTracker.CurLifeStageIndex`\n-- PawnSafe Class C, which on a stale cache can rename the pawn and add/remove\ncomponents. It is a roster cosmetic, and the substantive gates (EverWork, a\nnon-null timetable, a non-null tracker) already exclude every pawn it would have.\nNamed in the header rather than left as an omission.\n\n6. ANOMALY KNOWLEDGE PROJECTS ARE REFUSED by `research-set`/`research-stop`, for\ntwo independent reasons carried in the result: DESIGN's non-goals put\nDLC-specific colony management out of v1, and SetCurrentProject's second half\nwalks `CurrentAnomalyKnowledgeProjects`, whose getter ADDS to a scribed list --\nthe trap 2.4's `research` observer already declines to touch. Keeping one rule\nacross observe and act. Their baseCost is 0 anyway, so the first half would\nsilently not set them: the verb would have reported success with nothing done.\n\n7. RELEASE IS A MODE, NOT A FLAG. The spec body said \"recruit toggle, release\".\n`Pawn_GuestTracker.Released` is written by the release JOB, not the player; the\nplayer's control is the exclusive interaction mode `Release`, after which a warden\nwalks the prisoner out. Writing `Released` directly would be a god-hand that\nskips the job, so `warden` does not offer it.\n\n== FIVE FINDINGS ==\n\nA. A SIXTH FORCE-PAUSING-MODAL SITE, and a grep for the tutorial helper does not\nfind it. The orchestrator's note named five `DoModalDialogIfNotKnown` call sites.\nThere is another route entirely:\n`HealthCardUtility.CreateSurgeryBill(..., sendMessages:true)` calls\n`Bill.CreateNoPawnsWithSkillDialog`, which is a bare\n`Find.WindowStack.Add(new Dialog_MessageBox(text))`, whenever no free colonist\nmeets the recipe's skill requirement. That is a modal raised by the ORDINARY\nbill-creation path on an input an agent hits constantly (\"queue the bionic before\nanyone can install it\"), and per 1.7 it wedges every later advance permanently.\nBoth of my call sites now pass sendMessages:false and RE-DERIVE the four warnings\nas result fields (`warnings:[{key,text,...}]`) -- better information than a\ntop-of-screen message the agent never reads. Vanilla already treats the path as\nunsafe unattended: `Pawn_GuestTracker.GuestTrackerTickInterval`'s own auto-bill\ncall passes false. **Suggest re-grepping for `new Dialog_MessageBox` rather than\nfor the tutorial helper; 3.5 will want the full list.**\n\nB. THE COLD-OUTFIT ACCEPTANCE BULLET'S IMPLIED MECHANISM DOES NOT EXIST.\n`dev:weather` cannot make a pawn dress warm. `JobGiver_OptimizeApparel`'s\n`neededWarmth` comes from `PawnApparelGenerator.CalculateNeededWarmth`, which is\n`GenTemperature.AverageTemperatureAtTileForTwelfth(tile, twelfth)` -- the tile's\nSEASONAL average. Weather moves rain, wind and accuracy; it does not move that by\none degree. The loop that IS deterministic is filter-driven: the apparel setter\nfires `Notify_OutfitChanged` (sets `nextApparelOptimizeTick = now`), the first\npass in the job giver issues `RemoveApparel` for every worn item the new filter\ndisallows unconditionally, and `ApparelScoreGain` then multiplies a\nnon-conflicting candidate by 10. So the fixture is \"a policy allowing only warm\napparel, with that apparel IN STORAGE\" (`IsInAnyStorage()` is a hard skip in the\ncandidate loop) and it is season-independent. The acceptance script does it that\nway and says why. **The spec text is stale on this point.**\n\nC. `prioritizeSustains` in Core is the DOCTOR, BILL-WORKER and CONSTRUCTION\ngivers -- `DoBills*`, `ConstructFinishFrames`, `Construct/DeliverResourcesTo*`,\n`FixBrokenDownBuilding`, `Refuel`, `RearmTurrets`, `Train`, `TakeToPen`, all the\n`DoctorTend*`/`DoctorFeed*` -- and NOT mining or sowing, which is the plausible\nguess. `HaulGeneral` does not set it, so the acceptance bullet's own\n`prioritize haul` can only verify the result's honesty about NOT writing durable\nstate; the write itself is exercised separately with `DoctorTendToHumanlikes`,\nfollowed by a draft to show `Pawn_DraftController`'s setter clearing it.\n\nD. `Pawn_ReadingTracker` HAS THE SAME LAZY-INIT TRAP AS THE OTHER THREE POLICY\nTRACKERS AND PawnSafe DOES NOT COVER IT. `CurrentPolicy` does\n`if (curPolicy == null) curPolicy = ...DefaultReadingPolicy();` and `curPolicy` is\n`Scribe_References.Look(ref curPolicy, \"curAssignment\")` -- so reading a reading\npolicy ASSIGNS one forever, exactly like the outfit/food/drug getters PawnSafe\nClass A documents. The reading database postdates 2.2. I put the guarded\nbacking-field route in my own file (`PawnActs.ReadingPolicyOf`) rather than edit a\nshipped file mid-parallel-worker, and left an ORCHESTRATOR note at it: fold it\ninto `PawnSafe.Policies` at merge if you want one vocabulary in one place. The\nfield name is `reading`.\n\nE. NO PYTHON ON BORGES, so no rwa-driven acceptance is possible here. RUNLOG\nsession 5 already records it; restating because it shaped the deliverable. The\nacceptance runner is PowerShell over the RAW FILE PROTOCOL\n(`commands/\u003cid\u003e.json` in, `results/\u003cid\u003e.json` out) rather than rwa, so it runs on\nthis box today: `pwsh accept\\3.4-pawn-orders.ps1`. 78 envelopes, 138 checks,\n`-DryRun` exercised end to end. Every envelope is also written out in\n`accept/3.4-pawn-orders.md` so any other client can drive it.\n\n== CROSS-SPEC, AS DISPATCHED ==\n\n`flick` NOT implemented (3.2's). Area CREATION and cell painting NOT implemented\n(3.2's, per the mid-task ruling); `assign {pawns, area}` is the pawn-side\nassignment only, gated on `PawnColumnWorker_AllowedArea`'s clauses plus\n`Area.AssignableAsAllowed()`, and an unknown area's error names 3.2 as the owner\nof creation. Acceptance needs no non-Home area: `Area_Home` overrides\n`AssignableAsAllowed` to true, so the assign path is exercisable today.\nJOURNAL.md untouched; the `action` emitter is a `private static` on my own\npartial class (`PawnActs`), so it cannot collide with 3.2's at merge.","files":null}]}